it's back-breaking work barefoot and  without so much as gloves to protect  themselves workers are taking freighters  apart  one of the biggest so-called ship  cemeteries in Chittagong the smoke  billowing from the blow torches makes it  hard to breathe  Mohammed Alamgir has worked here since  he was just nine years old our lives are  in our own hands they don't care about  us they don't want to take care of us if  they paid us more they'd make less money  for themselves that's why we barely get  anything Alamgir makes just five euros a  day around 20,000 people work here  although it's dangerous work none of  them have accident or health insurance  and that's despite Salvage firms making  about 1 million euros per ship accidents  are very common ships are stripped down  to a steel shell which has then taken  apart by a swarm of workers armed with  blow torches the metal is taken ashore  and melted down for Alamgir it's a 70  hour working week if I don't do this  work I'll have nothing to eat I won't be  able to take care of my family that's  why I'm forced to do this if I missed a  single day of work I wouldn't have  enough money to last the month but  there's nothing I can do about it I'll  carry on for as long as my body can  endure it we don't have any money and my  father is unemployed because he can't  work he'll be very angry with me if I  don't work there's just nowhere to make  money  the International Maritime Organization  curved the dangerous job of processing  scrap in its Hong Kong Convention but  that's largely been ignored having only  been ratified by Norway Congo and France
 

